    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Decomposition:</strong> Students often define this as "breaking down a program." It must be understood as breaking down the problem before any code exists.</li>
            <li><strong>Abstraction:</strong> Misunderstood as "making things complicated." It must be taught as "removing irrelevant detail" to focus on the core logic.</li>
            <li><strong>Flowcharts:</strong> Forgetting to label decision branches (True/False) or leaving flowlines disconnected.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Knowledge & Maps:</strong> "The map is not the territory." How does abstraction change our knowledge of the real world? (e.g., the London Tube Map vs. real geography).</li>
                <li><strong>Naming as Power:</strong> Does naming a problem in a specification "freeze" it into a category that limits our creative solutions?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

